(iii) dreamed speed / true speed (the law says 1.000)
       tau        0       24       60      120      199   horizon
      0.00    1.069    1.013    1.082    1.635    1.564      11.3
      0.50    1.502    4.092    6.275    6.771    9.809       0.9
      1.00    8.683    9.797   12.565   12.324   14.829       0.3
     floor    1.000    1.000    1.000    1.000      nan      41.4
    'horizon' = mean first step at which the dreamed speed is off the law by > 25% (max 200)
    At tau > 0 the RATIO is inflated by per-step sampling jitter that the position
    probe cannot separate from motion. 
    (the across-episode speed correlation is undefined here: in v1 every
    episode has the SAME true speed, so there is no variance to correlate with.)

(v) v3: the HIDDEN ball's velocity across a dreamed occlusion.
    Nothing inside the band can turn a ball around, so vy must keep its sign and
    |v| and |vx| must be unchanged. This compares the dream with ITSELF, so it is
    the one metric here that is meaningful at tau = 1.
       tau  stretches  vy sign kept  |v| out/in  |vx| out/in  vx flipped
      0.00        248         0.484       1.102        1.034       0.492
      0.50        547         0.391       1.077        1.038       0.488
      1.00         54         0.537       1.255        0.925       0.444
     floor         90         0.989       1.000        1.000       0.267
    ('floor' is the same estimator on TRUE latents: the instrument's own score.)

(iv) fraction of dreamed frames with a well-formed ball (area within 50-150% of the VAE reference)
    tau=0.00  mean over the dream 0.153   at the end 0.000
    tau=0.50  mean over the dream 0.091   at the end 0.033
    tau=1.00  mean over the dream 0.368   at the end 0.333

In plain English:
  At step 150 the dreamed ball moves at 12.65 x the speed the law gives it.
  The dream stays inside +-25% of the law for 0 steps on average (the
  measuring probe's own floor is 41).  37% of the dreamed frames still
  contain a ball of roughly the right size.
  At tau=0.0 (deterministic) the same numbers are speed ratio 1.27, speed horizon 11.
  The gap between the two rows is the cost of sampling, which is the whole
  finding: the model KNOWS the quantity is constant, and loses it anyway
  as soon as it has to feed on its own draws.

